# The keys and the patient records must not be uploaded. All of them are
# already git-ignored; named again here because this directory is deployed
# directly, and "another file covers it" is not a good enough reason for a
# key to stay on this machine.
#
# The glob matters: .env holds the provider key and .env.local holds the
# OIDC token Vercel itself wrote here. Matching only ".env" would upload
# the second one.
.env*
.vercel/
*.db
.venv/
__pycache__/
app/__pycache__/

# Test suites. They run locally, and verify_records/verify_store_dump import
# the database layer this deployment deliberately does not have.
scripts/

# Procfile names `app.main:app` — the FULL district service, record endpoints
# and all. It exists for hosts that read it (Render, Railway) and is correct
# there, where the service sits on a trusted network.
#
# Vercel reads it too, and treats its presence as "this project is a service",
# which silently overrides the api/ directory and deploys main.py instead of
# the assistant-only entry point. Excluded so this deployment can only ever be
# what api/index.py says it is.
Procfile

# app/main.py is the district service. Excluded from THIS deployment entirely,
# for two reasons.
#
# The first is detection: Vercel scans for an ASGI app and finds main.py before
# it looks at api/, then deploys the district service instead of the assistant
# — which promptly crashes, because its first act is to create a SQLite file on
# a read-only filesystem. The warning it prints ("the api/ directory will not
# be built") is easy to read past.
#
# The second is the reason that one is worth fixing properly rather than
# working around: the assistant imports app.chat_engine and app.schemas and
# nothing else, so main.py has no business being uploaded. Absent beats present
# and unrouted — there is no configuration mistake that can expose a record
# endpoint that was never shipped.
app/main.py
